\chapter{\texorpdfstring{PEG Combinators, \texttt{PEGMatch}, and the
\texttt{PEGMatcher}}{PEG Combinators, , and the }}\label{ch28-peg-combinators-pegmatch-and-the-pegmatcher}

Chapter 27 introduced the \texttt{PEGDefinition} grammar: an ordered set
of rules, each compiled from a
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern} regex subset, each
carrying an optional semantic action and a channel. This chapter
completes the PEG story. It describes the \texttt{PEGMatch} record that
the engine hands to every action, the \texttt{PEGMatcher} that walks an
input string arm by arm, and the \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}*} combinator
family that lifts the same grammar into the parser-combinator world of
Chapters 23 through 25.

Where Chapter 27 was about \emph{declaring} a grammar, this chapter is
about \emph{running} one. Two execution paths are presented. The first
is \texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse}, a
self-contained driver that applies ordered choice across all visible
rules until input is exhausted. The second is \texttt{PEGMatcher}, a
fluent, stream-style matcher whose
\texttt{operator\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textless{}\allowbreak{}}
consumes one match arm at a time and whose wildcard arm recovers from
otherwise unmatched input. Both paths share \texttt{PEGMatch} as their
reporting record and both honour the channel discipline that routes
ignore matches silently.

The chapter closes with the \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}seq},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}choice}, \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}opt},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many}, \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many1},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}and}, and \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}not}
combinators. These are thin, additive wrappers over the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Parser} machinery of Chapter 23,
re-expressed with strict PEG semantics: ordered choice, full
backtracking on partial failure, and zero-width lookahead. They allow a
grammar author to mix the rule-based PEG engine with the
combinator-based parser engine in a single program.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{PEGMatch}
Record}{The  Record}}\label{ch28-the-pegmatch-record}

Every semantic action in the PEG engine has the signature
\texttt{void(\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\&)\allowbreak{}}. The
\texttt{PEGMatch} is the engine's report of one successful match: the
text consumed, the offsets that bound it, the rule that produced it, the
channel it was routed to, and the line and column at which it begins. It
is the single channel through which a grammar author observes what the
engine did.

The structure is deliberately small and copyable. Its most important
member, \texttt{value}, is a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} into
the source text being parsed. The view is only valid for the lifetime of
that source text and for the duration of the parse that produced it; an
action that stores \texttt{value} for later inspection must copy the
characters into owning storage.

\begin{code}
struct PEGMatch
{
  std::string_view value{};   ///< matched text
  std::size_t begin{ 0 };     ///< start offset in source
  std::size_t end{ 0 };       ///< one-past-end offset in source
  PEGRule *rule{ nullptr };   ///< rule that produced this match
  PEGChannel channel{};       ///< resolved channel of the rule
  void *user_data{ nullptr }; ///< optional extension point

  std::size_t line{ 1 };      ///< 1-based line of `begin`
  std::size_t column{ 1 };    ///< 1-based column of `begin`
  bool ignored{ false };      ///< true if routed to @IGNORE

  /// @return length of the matched text (end - begin).
  std::size_t
  length () const noexcept
  {
    return end - begin;
  }
};
\end{code}

The \texttt{begin} and \texttt{end} members give the half-open offset
range in the source. \texttt{length(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is
exactly \texttt{end - begin}; it is the value that example 23 prints in
its \texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ws:\allowbreak{}} line. The \texttt{rule}
pointer names the \texttt{PEGRule} whose pattern produced the match; for
wildcard arms fired by the \texttt{PEGMatcher}, \texttt{rule} is
\texttt{nullptr}. The \texttt{channel} member is the resolved channel of
the rule at fire time, and \texttt{ignored} is a convenience boolean
equivalent to
\texttt{channel.\allowbreak{}is\_\allowbreak{}ignore(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.

The \texttt{user\_\allowbreak{}data} slot is an opaque extension point.
The engine never reads or writes it; it exists so that an embedding
application can attach auxiliary state to a match without subclassing
\texttt{PEGMatch} or threading separate context through every action. A
common use is to store a pointer to a token object that the action has
just allocated.

The \texttt{line} and \texttt{column} members are computed by
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}detail:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}line\_\allowbreak{}col\_\allowbreak{}at},
which scans the source text from offset zero up to \texttt{begin}
counting newlines. They are 1-based. Their cost is linear in
\texttt{begin}, which is acceptable for the line-oriented grammars the
PEG engine targets; an embedding that finds this cost significant should
maintain its own line cache.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Reading \texttt{value} and
\texttt{length}}{Reading  and }}\label{ch28-reading-value-and-length}

Example 23 in the distribution is the canonical demonstration of an
action reading a \texttt{PEGMatch}. The whitespace rule is registered
with the \texttt{@IGNORE} channel and its action prints the length
consumed; the keyword rules print their matched \texttt{value}.

\begin{code}
auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();
auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t\\n]+"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &match)
                                           { std::cout << "skip_ws:" << match.length () << "\n"; });
ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;

auto &kw_if = grammar.add_rule<"if"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &match)
                                      { std::cout << "kw:" << match.value << "\n"; });
\end{code}

Given the input \texttt{"if x1 + 2 then \allowbreak{}40"}, the engine
first matches \texttt{if} and prints \texttt{kw:\allowbreak{}if}. It
then matches the space, prints
\texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ws:\allowbreak{}1}, and continues. The
whitespace action fires even though the channel is \texttt{@IGNORE}; the
channel controls token \emph{emission}, not action invocation. This
distinction is fundamental and is revisited below.

The \texttt{value} view is exactly
\texttt{text.\allowbreak{}substr(\allowbreak{}begin,\allowbreak{} end - begin)\allowbreak{}}.
An action that needs the matched text as a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} writes
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string(\allowbreak{}match.\allowbreak{}value)\allowbreak{}}.
An action that needs the character at a specific offset uses
\texttt{match.\allowbreak{}value[i]} for \texttt{i} in
\texttt{[0,\allowbreak{} match.\allowbreak{}length(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
No bounds checking is performed beyond the inherent bounds of
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{rule}
Back-Reference}{The  Back-Reference}}\label{ch28-the-rule-back-reference}

The \texttt{rule} member lets an action inspect the rule that fired it.
This is useful when several rules share a single action function and the
action must distinguish between them, or when the action wants to read
the rule's \texttt{pattern\_\allowbreak{}text} for diagnostics. The
pointer is \texttt{const\_\allowbreak{}cast} away from \texttt{const}
only because the action signature takes a non-const reference; an action
should not mutate the rule's pattern or compiled form from within the
match.

The \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} hook described in Chapter 27 fires
a second rule's action when the primary rule matches. In that case the
primary match record carries the primary rule's pointer; the secondary
match record, constructed separately inside
\texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse}, carries the
\texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} rule's pointer. Both actions fire, in
that order, at the same source position.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{PEGMatcher}
Engine}{The  Engine}}\label{ch28-the-pegmatcher-engine}

The \texttt{PEGMatcher} is a fluent, stream-style matcher over a
\texttt{PEGDefinition} and a source string. Where
\texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse} runs the whole
input to completion in one call, \texttt{PEGMatcher} advances one match
at a time, giving the caller control over which rules to try and in what
order, at every position. It is the engine of choice when the grammar
author wants per-position customisation or wildcard recovery.

The matcher is created by
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}new\_\allowbreak{}matcher},
which binds a reference to the definition and a copy of the source view.
The caller then chains arms with
\texttt{operator\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textless{}\allowbreak{}} until
the input is spent, and finally calls
\texttt{close(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The
\texttt{close(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} call is a no-op preserved for
API symmetry with stream-style interfaces; it releases nothing because
the matcher holds nothing that needs releasing.

\begin{code}
struct PEGMatcher
{
  const PEGDefinition *def{ nullptr };
  std::string_view text{};
  std::size_t pos{ 0 };

  bool
  is_spent () const noexcept
  {
    return pos >= text.size ();
  }
  std::size_t
  position () const noexcept
  {
    return pos;
  }
  /// No-op for API symmetry; releases nothing (nothing is held).
  void
  close () noexcept
  {
  }
  // ...
};
\end{code}

The public state is the cursor \texttt{pos}. The matcher advances
\texttt{pos} only when an arm fires. Until then the cursor is stationary
and the accumulated arms are retained for the next
\texttt{step(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. This retention is what allows
arms to be chained across multiple
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textless{}\allowbreak{}} calls within a
single source position: the matcher waits until one of them matches
before consuming input.

The matcher does not own the \texttt{PEGDefinition} it references. The
definition must outlive the matcher. Because
\texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}add\_\allowbreak{}rule}
returns references that are stable across appends, a common pattern is
to build the grammar once, store it in a long-lived object, and create a
fresh \texttt{PEGMatcher} for each input string.

\subsection{The Match Arm}\label{ch28-the-match-arm}

An arm is a lightweight record consumed by
\texttt{operator\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textless{}\allowbreak{}}. It is
constructed by calling a \texttt{PEGRule} with a callback, which yields
a \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}detail:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}PEGArm}
bound to that rule and that action. The matcher also provides a
\texttt{wildcard(\allowbreak{}Fn)\allowbreak{}} helper that produces an
arm with \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}wildcard} set and no rule attached.

\begin{code}
auto m = dsl::peg_new_matcher (grammar, source);
while (!m.is_spent ())
  {
    m << keyword
        ([&] (dsl::PEGMatch &match) { std::cout << "[" << match.value << "]\n"; })
      << number
             ([&] (dsl::PEGMatch &match) { std::cout << "[" << match.value << "]\n"; })
      << ident ([&] (dsl::PEGMatch &match) { std::cout << "[" << match.value << "]\n"; })
      << m.wildcard ([&] (dsl::PEGMatch &match)
                     { std::cout << "[unknown:'" << match.value << "']"; });
  }
m.close ();
\end{code}

Each \texttt{rule(\allowbreak{}callback)\allowbreak{}} expression
produces a fresh arm. Arms accumulate in the matcher until one fires, at
which point the accumulator is cleared and the cursor advances. If no
rule arm matches at the current position, the first wildcard arm in the
accumulator fires and consumes exactly one character. This is the
recovery mechanism: a wildcard arm at the end of the chain guarantees
forward progress.

The \texttt{PEGArm} structure carries a \texttt{rule} pointer, an
\texttt{action}, the \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}wildcard} flag, and an
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}channel} flag with an attached
\texttt{PEGChannel}. Channel arms are produced by the matcher's
channel-filter API and match any rule whose channel name agrees; they
are described below.

\subsection{Ordered Choice Across
Arms}\label{ch28-ordered-choice-across-arms}

The \texttt{step(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method is the heart of the
matcher. It first skips ignored-channel matches, then walks the
accumulated arms in order. The first arm whose rule matches at the
current position fires its action, advances the cursor, and clears the
accumulator. This is ordered choice at the arm level: the order in which
arms are streamed into the matcher is the order of precedence.

\begin{code}
void
step ()
{
  if (arms_.empty () || is_spent ())
    return;
  skip_ignored ();
  if (is_spent ())
    {
      arms_.clear ();
      return;
    }
  // Non-wildcard arms in order: first match wins.
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < arms_.size (); ++i)
    {
      const auto &arm = arms_[i];
      if (arm.is_wildcard)
        continue;
      // ... channel and rule branches ...
      if (auto end = arm.rule->try_match (text, pos))
        {
          fire (arm.action, arm.rule, pos, *end);
          arms_.clear ();
          return;
        }
    }
  // No rule arm matched: fire the first wildcard arm, if any.
  // ...
}
\end{code}

The arms list is walked twice. The first walk skips wildcards and tests
each rule arm. The second walk fires the first wildcard arm if no rule
arm matched. This ordering means that a wildcard placed in the middle of
the chain does not preempt later rule arms; only when \emph{all} rule
arms have failed does the wildcard fire. A chain with no wildcard and no
matching rule arm leaves the accumulator intact and the cursor
stationary, waiting for the next
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textless{}\allowbreak{}} to supply a
further arm.

This last behaviour is subtle. It permits a caller to assemble an arm
set incrementally across several statements, only committing to a step
once a matching arm has been streamed. It also means that a chain with
no wildcard and no match will loop forever if the caller keeps
re-streaming the same arms; the caller must either include a wildcard or
break out of the loop on a stationary cursor.

\subsection{Ignored-Channel Skipping}\label{ch28-ignored-channel-skipping}

Before any arm is tested, \texttt{step(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
calls \texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ignored(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
This method walks every rule in the definition whose channel is
\texttt{@IGNORE} and applies it at the current position. If any such
rule matches and consumes at least one character, the cursor advances
and the walk repeats. The loop terminates when no ignore rule makes
progress.

\begin{code}
void
skip_ignored ()
{
  bool progressed = true;
  while (progressed && !is_spent ())
    {
      progressed = false;
      for (const PEGRule *r : def->rules ())
        {
          if (!r->channel.is_ignore ())
            continue;
          if (auto end = r->try_match (text, pos))
            {
              if (*end == pos)
                continue;
              pos = *end;
              progressed = true;
              break;
            }
        }
    }
}
\end{code}

The zero-width check (\texttt{*end == pos}) is critical. An ignore rule
that matches the empty string would otherwise loop forever, advancing
the cursor by zero on each iteration. The matcher explicitly skips such
matches and continues to the next rule. The same zero-width protection
appears in \texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse},
where it aborts the parse with an explicit error rather than silently
looping.

Note that \texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ignored} does \emph{not} fire
ignore-rule actions. It advances the cursor silently. This is a
deliberate choice for the matcher path: the matcher's arms own the
actions for the positions they match, and ignore matches are treated as
pure whitespace stripping. The \texttt{parse} driver, by contrast, does
fire ignore-rule actions, which is why example 23's
\texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ws:\allowbreak{}} line appears in its output.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{fire}
Helper}{The  Helper}}\label{ch28-the-fire-helper}

When an arm matches, \texttt{step(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} delegates
to \texttt{fire(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} to construct the
\texttt{PEGMatch} record and invoke the action. The helper fills every
field of the record, advances the cursor to the match end, and then
calls either the arm's own action (if one was supplied with the arm) or
the rule's stored semantic action.

\begin{code}
void
fire (const PEGSemanticAction &action, const PEGRule *rule,
      std::size_t begin, std::size_t end)
{
  PEGMatch m;
  m.value = text.substr (begin, end - begin);
  m.begin = begin;
  m.end = end;
  m.rule = const_cast<PEGRule *> (rule);
  m.channel = rule->channel;
  m.ignored = rule->channel.is_ignore ();
  peg_detail::line_col_at (text, begin, m.line, m.column);
  pos = end;
  if (action)
    action (m);
  else if (rule->semantic_action)
    rule->semantic_action (m);
}
\end{code}

The precedence between arm action and rule action is significant. An arm
constructed as \texttt{rule(\allowbreak{}callback)\allowbreak{}} carries
its own action, which overrides the rule's stored semantic action for
that arm. An arm constructed without a callback would fall back to the
rule's stored action. In practice every arm supplies a callback, but the
fallback exists so that a rule authored with a strong action can be
reused in a matcher without re-specifying the action.

The cursor is advanced before the action runs. An action that inspects
\texttt{m.\allowbreak{}end} therefore sees the same value the matcher
will use for the next position. An action that wishes to ``reject'' a
match after the fact cannot do so by mutating the match record; the
engine offers no backtracking channel out of an action. If an action
needs to suppress a match, it must do so by cooperating with the grammar
structure, for example by routing the rule to a channel the caller
filters out.

\subsection{The Wildcard Arm}\label{ch28-the-wildcard-arm}

When no rule arm matches, \texttt{step(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
walks the accumulator a second time looking for the first wildcard arm.
The wildcard constructs a \texttt{PEGMatch} covering exactly one
character at the current position, fires the wildcard's action, advances
the cursor by one, and clears the accumulator.

\begin{code}
PEGMatch m;
m.value = text.substr (pos, 1);
m.begin = pos;
m.end = pos + 1;
m.rule = nullptr;
peg_detail::line_col_at (text, pos, m.line, m.column);
if (arms_[i].action)
  arms_[i].action (m);
++pos; // wildcard consumes one character
arms_.clear ();
return;
\end{code}

The wildcard's \texttt{rule} pointer is \texttt{nullptr}, which lets an
action distinguish a wildcard fire from a rule fire. The wildcard
consumes exactly one character regardless of what that character is;
this is the matcher's guarantee of forward progress. A chain that ends
with a wildcard never stalls and never needs an external break
condition.

The wildcard is the matcher's error-recovery mechanism. In a tokenizer,
a wildcard arm typically emits a ``unknown character'' diagnostic and
continues, so that a single unexpected character does not abort
tokenization of the rest of the input. The \texttt{parse} driver has no
equivalent; it stops at the first unmatched character.

\section{Channel Filtering}\label{ch28-channel-filtering}

The \texttt{PEGMatcher} provides three channel-filter mutators:
\texttt{only}, \texttt{include}, and \texttt{exclude}. Each returns a
reference to the matcher so that it can be chained before the matching
loop. The filter restricts which rule arms are considered by
\texttt{step(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}; it does not affect
\texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ignored}, which always considers all ignore
rules regardless of filter.

\begin{code}
PEGMatcher &
only (std::string_view channel)
{
  mode_ = FilterMode::Only;
  channels_.clear ();
  channels_.push_back (std::string (channel));
  return *this;
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{only} mode restricts matching to rules on a single named
channel. The \texttt{include} mode adds a channel to the accepted set,
switching from \texttt{All} to \texttt{Only} if necessary. The
\texttt{exclude} mode rejects rules on the named channel, accepting all
others. The \texttt{channel\_\allowbreak{}allowed} predicate encodes
these semantics: in \texttt{Only} mode a channel is allowed iff it
appears in the list; in \texttt{Exclude} mode iff it does not.

Channel filters are evaluated against the rule's resolved channel name.
A rule on \texttt{@DEFAULT} is allowed under
\texttt{only(\allowbreak{}"@DEFAULT")\allowbreak{}} and excluded under
\texttt{exclude(\allowbreak{}"@DEFAULT")\allowbreak{}}. Ignore rules are
always skipped by \texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ignored} before the filter
is consulted, so filtering \texttt{@IGNORE} has no effect on the
matcher's behaviour; the ignore rules still run.

Example 24 in the distribution uses
\texttt{only(\allowbreak{}"@DEFAULT")\allowbreak{}} to restrict a
matcher to ordinary tokens. This is a common pattern when a grammar
defines comment or whitespace rules on \texttt{@IGNORE} and the caller
wants to enumerate only the semantic tokens. The filter is set once
before the loop and applies to every step.

\subsection{Channel Arms}\label{ch28-channel-arms}

In addition to filtering, the matcher supports channel \emph{arms}. A
channel arm is constructed by the matcher's internal channel-arm
machinery and matches any rule on a named channel, in declaration order.
When \texttt{step(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} encounters a channel arm,
it walks the definition's rules looking for the first one whose channel
name matches and whose pattern matches at the current position.

\begin{code}
if (arm.is_channel)
  {
    for (const PEGRule *r : def->rules ())
      {
        if (r->channel.name != arm.channel.name)
          continue;
        if (auto end = r->try_match (text, pos))
          {
            fire (arm.action, r, pos, *end);
            arms_.clear ();
            return;
          }
      }
    continue;
  }
\end{code}

A channel arm is a concise way to say ``match whatever token is next on
this channel'' without enumerating the individual rules. It is the
channel analogue of the wildcard: where the wildcard matches any single
character, a channel arm matches any single rule on its channel. Channel
arms compose with rule arms in the same accumulator and obey the same
first-match-wins ordering.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{parse}
Driver}{The  Driver}}\label{ch28-the-parse-driver}

The \texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse} method is
the all-in-one driver. It takes a source string, walks it from offset
zero, and at each position tries every visible rule in order until one
matches. On success it advances the cursor and continues; on failure it
records the offset, line, and column and returns. It succeeds when the
entire input is consumed.

\begin{code}
PEGParseResult
parse (std::string_view text) const
{
  PEGParseResult result;
  auto all = rules ();
  std::size_t pos = 0;
  while (pos < text.size ())
    {
      bool matched = false;
      for (const PEGRule *r : all)
        {
          auto end = r->try_match (text, pos);
          if (!end)
            continue;
          // ... fire action, advance, break ...
        }
      // ...
    }
  result.offset = pos;
  return result;
}
\end{code}

The driver has no wildcard recovery. If no rule matches at the current
position, the parse fails immediately. The error record carries the
offset, the 1-based line and column, and a message of the form
\texttt{"no PEG rule mat\allowbreak{}ched at offset N\allowbreak{}"}.
The caller inspects
\texttt{result.\allowbreak{}ok(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{result.\allowbreak{}failed(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{result.\allowbreak{}error\_\allowbreak{}offset(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{result.\allowbreak{}error\_\allowbreak{}line(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{result.\allowbreak{}error\_\allowbreak{}column(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
and
\texttt{result.\allowbreak{}error\_\allowbreak{}message(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
to diagnose the failure.

The driver fires ignore-rule actions in line with non-ignore actions.
The channel does not suppress action invocation; it only marks the match
as ignored. An action that wishes to skip work on ignored matches checks
\texttt{match.\allowbreak{}ignored} or
\texttt{match.\allowbreak{}channel.\allowbreak{}is\_\allowbreak{}ignore(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
and returns early.

\subsection{Zero-Width Protection}\label{ch28-zero-width-protection}

The parse driver explicitly rejects zero-width positive matches. If a
rule matches and consumes no input, the driver sets \texttt{success} to
false, records the position, and returns with the message
\texttt{"PEG rule matche\allowbreak{}d empty input; r\allowbreak{}efusing to loop"\allowbreak{}}.
This is a hard error, not a warning: the parse cannot continue because
the cursor would not advance.

\begin{code}
if (!negated && *end == pos)
  {
    result.success = false;
    result.offset = pos;
    peg_detail::line_col_at (text, pos, result.line, result.column);
    result.message = "PEG rule matched empty input; refusing to loop";
    return result;
  }
\end{code}

This protection is essential for patterns that can match the empty
string, such as \texttt{[a-z]*} applied at a position where no letter
follows. A pattern of that form is almost always a grammar bug. The
library refuses to guess whether the author intended a \texttt{+}
quantifier or an explicit lower bound; it reports the situation and lets
the author fix the grammar.

Negated rules (\texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail}) are exempt from this
check because their success consumes one character by construction, as
described in Chapter 27. The \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} hook is
also exempt because it runs at the same position as the primary rule and
does not independently advance the cursor.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed}
Hook}{The  Hook}}\label{ch28-the-if_succeed-hook}

When a rule with an \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} hook matches, the
driver attempts the hooked rule at the same position. If the hooked rule
also matches, its action fires with a match record covering its own
span. The cursor then advances to the \emph{primary} rule's end, not the
hooked rule's end; the hook is a side channel for additional matching,
not a sequence operator.

\begin{code}
if (r->if_succeed)
  {
    if (auto e2 = r->if_succeed->try_match (text, pos))
      {
        PEGMatch m;
        m.value = text.substr (pos, *e2 - pos);
        m.begin = pos;
        m.end = *e2;
        m.rule = r->if_succeed;
        m.channel = r->if_succeed->channel;
        m.ignored = r->if_succeed->channel.is_ignore ();
        peg_detail::line_col_at (text, pos, m.line, m.column);
        if (r->if_succeed->semantic_action)
          r->if_succeed->semantic_action (m);
      }
  }
pos = *end;
\end{code}

A typical use of \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} is to attach a
lookahead-style action: when a keyword matches, the hook can inspect
what follows without consuming it. The hook's match record covers the
lookahead span, but the cursor advance uses the primary match's
\texttt{end}. If the hook fails, the primary match still stands and the
cursor still advances; the hook is best-effort.

The action firing order is therefore: primary action first, then
\texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} action. Both fire at the same source
position. The wildcard arm in the \texttt{PEGMatcher} does not support
\texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed}; the hook is a property of
\texttt{PEGRule} and the matcher's wildcard has no rule.

\section{Derived Grammars and Rule
Ordering}\label{ch28-derived-grammars-and-rule-ordering}

Chapter 27 introduced
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}derive\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}definition}
for grammar inheritance. The derived definition sees parent rules first,
then its own local rules, in that order. The
\texttt{rules(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method assembles this list by
recursing through the \texttt{parent} pointer.

\begin{code}
std::vector<const PEGRule *>
rules () const
{
  std::vector<const PEGRule *> out;
  if (parent)
    out = parent->rules ();
  for (const auto &r : local_rules_)
    out.push_back (&r);
  return out;
}
\end{code}

Because ordered choice takes the first matching rule, parent rules take
precedence over child rules at any decision point. A child grammar that
wishes to override a parent rule must add a more specific rule that
matches before the parent rule is reached. Because the parent's rules
come first in the list, a child cannot override by simple redeclaration;
it must shadow by writing a rule that matches a strict superset of the
parent's pattern and that appears earlier in the list.

In practice, derived grammars are usually additive: the parent defines
the common tokens and the child adds new ones. Example 23 derives a
grammar that adds a \texttt{0x[0-9a-fA-F]+} hex literal rule to the base
keyword-and-identifier grammar. The derived grammar parses input that
the base grammar cannot, while still recognising everything the base
grammar recognises.

The \texttt{PEGMatcher} respects derivation automatically because it
queries
\texttt{def-\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}rules(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
for both \texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ignored} and channel-arm matching. A
matcher over a derived grammar sees the same merged rule list that
\texttt{parse} sees. There is no separate API for derived matchers; the
same \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}new\_\allowbreak{}matcher} works for both
root and derived definitions.

\section{The PEG Combinator Family}\label{ch28-the-peg-combinator-family}

The PEG engine described so far is rule-based: patterns are compiled at
runtime from string literals. The library also provides a combinator
family that expresses the same PEG semantics through the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Parser} machinery of Chapter 23.
These combinators are thin, additive wrappers; they do not replace the
parser combinators but re-express them with strict PEG semantics for use
in grammars that mix the two styles.

The combinators live in the \texttt{dsl} namespace and all produce
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(or, for the lookahead operators,
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}bool\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}).
They take
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
arguments and return
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
so they compose with each other and with the primitive parsers of
Chapter 24. The \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}rule} adapter converts a
\texttt{PEGRule} into a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
bridging the rule-based and combinator-based worlds.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Sequence:
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}seq}}{Sequence: }}\label{ch28-sequence-peg_seq}

The sequence combinator parses its arguments in order and fails if any
of them fails. On success it returns a \texttt{PEGMatch} whose span
covers the entire sequence. The combinator is variadic:
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}seq(\allowbreak{}a,\allowbreak{} b,\allowbreak{} c)\allowbreak{}}
parses \texttt{a}, then \texttt{b}, then \texttt{c}, and returns the
concatenated span.

\begin{code}
template <typename A, typename B, typename... Rest>
auto
peg_seq (const Parser<A> &a, const Parser<B> &b, const Rest &...rest)
{
  auto ab = parser ([a, b] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<PEGMatch> {
    std::size_t begin = in.pos;
    auto ra = a (in);
    if (!ra)
      return ExpectedResult<PEGMatch>::failure (ra.error.pos, ra.error.kind,
                                                ra.error.expected);
    auto rb = b (in);
    if (!rb)
      {
        bool consumed = in.pos != begin;
        in.pos = begin;
        auto kind = rb.error.kind;
        if (consumed)
          kind = ParseFailureKind::Committed;
        return ExpectedResult<PEGMatch>::failure (rb.error.pos, kind,
                                                  rb.error.expected);
      }
    PEGMatch m;
    m.begin = begin;
    m.end = in.pos;
    m.value = in.get_span (begin);
    return m;
  });
  if constexpr (sizeof...(rest) == 0)
    return ab;
  else
    return peg_seq (ab, rest...);
}
\end{code}

The key behaviour is the backtracking on failure. If \texttt{b} fails
after \texttt{a} succeeded, the cursor is restored to \texttt{begin}
before the failure is reported. The failure kind is upgraded to
\texttt{Committed} if \texttt{a} consumed input, signalling to an outer
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse} that the failure is not recoverable at
this position. This is the PEG sequence semantics: a sequence commits
once it has consumed input, and a later failure cannot be backtracked
over by an enclosing choice.

The recursive expansion folds the variadic list left-to-right.
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}seq(\allowbreak{}a,\allowbreak{} b,\allowbreak{} c)\allowbreak{}}
becomes
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}seq(\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}seq(\allowbreak{}a,\allowbreak{} b)\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} c)\allowbreak{}}.
The leftmost argument is parsed first and the rightmost last, matching
the reading order of a grammar rule \texttt{a b c}.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Ordered Choice:
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}choice}}{Ordered Choice: }}\label{ch28-ordered-choice-peg_choice}

The choice combinator implements PEG ordered choice. Each alternative is
wrapped in \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse}, which converts a committed
failure back into a soft failure that the choice can backtrack over. The
first alternative that succeeds wins; later alternatives are not tried
at that decision point.

\begin{code}
template <typename A, typename B, typename... Rest>
auto
peg_choice (const Parser<A> &a, const Parser<B> &b, const Rest &...rest)
{
  auto ab = try_parse (a) | try_parse (b);
  if constexpr (sizeof...(rest) == 0)
    return ab;
  else
    return peg_choice (ab, rest...);
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse} wrapper is the bridge between the
parser-combinator error model of Chapter 25 and the PEG backtracking
model. Without it, a committed failure from one alternative would
propagate through the \texttt{|} operator and abort the choice. With it,
every alternative is given a fresh chance at the decision point, and
only a truly soft failure (one that consumed no input) is recoverable.

The choice combinator is the analogue of the \texttt{PEGMatcher}'s
ordered arm list and of the
\texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse} driver's rule
loop. All three implement the same first-match-wins semantics. The
combinator form is useful when the alternatives are themselves
combinator expressions rather than compiled pattern strings.

\subsection{Optional and Repetition}\label{ch28-optional-and-repetition}

Three repetition combinators cover the standard PEG quantifiers.
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}opt} implements \texttt{a?}: it never fails
and never commits, matching zero or one \texttt{a}.
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many} implements \texttt{a*}: greedy
zero-or-more, with full backtracking on a partial failure.
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many1} implements \texttt{a+}: greedy
one-or-more, failing if the first iteration fails.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
auto
peg_opt (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return optional (try_parse (p));
}

template <typename T>
auto
peg_many (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return *try_parse (p);
}

template <typename T>
auto
peg_many1 (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return p & *try_parse (p);
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse} inside
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}opt} and \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many}
ensures that a partial failure of \texttt{p} does not commit the
surrounding context. A
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many(\allowbreak{}p)\allowbreak{}} that
matches three iterations and then fails on the fourth backtracks the
cursor to the end of the third iteration and reports success with three
matches. This is the PEG repetition semantics: greedily match as many as
possible, then stop.

The \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many1} form is
\texttt{p \& *try\_\allowbreak{}parse(\allowbreak{}p)\allowbreak{}}: one
unguarded \texttt{p} followed by zero-or-more guarded \texttt{p}. The
first \texttt{p} must succeed for the whole expression to succeed. If
the first \texttt{p} fails, the failure propagates with whatever kind
\texttt{p} produced; if a later \texttt{p} fails, the
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse} wrapper makes the failure recoverable
and the repetition terminates.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Lookahead: \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}and}
and
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}not}}{Lookahead:  and }}\label{ch28-lookahead-peg_and-and-peg_not}

The two lookahead combinators implement zero-width assertions.
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}and} succeeds if its argument matches and
consumes nothing. \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}not} succeeds if its
argument fails and consumes nothing. Both restore the cursor to its
saved position regardless of outcome.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
auto
peg_and (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return parser ([p] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<bool> {
    auto save = in.pos;
    auto r = p (in);
    in.pos = save;
    if (!r)
      return ExpectedResult<bool>::failure (r.error.pos, ParseFailureKind::Soft,
                                            r.error.expected);
    return true;
  });
}

template <typename T>
auto
peg_not (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return parser ([p] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<bool> {
    auto save = in.pos;
    auto r = p (in);
    in.pos = save;
    if (r)
      return fail_expected<bool> (in, "negative lookahead");
    return true;
  });
}
\end{code}

Lookahead is the PEG mechanism for context-sensitive matching.
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}and(\allowbreak{}digit)\allowbreak{}} asserts
that the next character is a digit without consuming it;
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}not(\allowbreak{}ch(\allowbreak{}'-')\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
asserts that the next character is not a minus. Both report their result
as
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}bool\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
not
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
because they consume no input and have no span to report.

Example 22 in the distribution uses \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}and} to
assert that an identifier begins with a letter, then
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many1} to consume the alphanumerics. The same
example uses \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}not} to build a ``signed number
that is not negative'' parser, asserting the absence of a minus sign
before consuming digits.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Bridging Rules and Combinators:
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}rule}}{Bridging Rules and Combinators: }}\label{ch28-bridging-rules-and-combinators-peg_rule}

The \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}rule} adapter wraps a \texttt{PEGRule} in
a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The resulting parser can be composed with the other
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}*} combinators exactly as if it were a
primitive parser. The adapter honours the rule's
\texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail} flag and resolves the match's channel
and \texttt{ignored} flag from the rule.

\begin{code}
inline Parser<PEGMatch>
peg_rule (const PEGRule &rule)
{
  return parser ([&rule] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<PEGMatch> {
    std::size_t begin = in.pos;
    auto end = rule.try_match (in.source, begin);
    if (!end)
      return fail_expected<PEGMatch> (in, rule.pattern_text);
    in.pos = *end;
    PEGMatch m;
    m.begin = begin;
    m.end = *end;
    m.value = in.source.substr (begin, *end - begin);
    m.rule = const_cast<PEGRule *> (&rule);
    m.channel = rule.channel;
    m.ignored = rule.channel.is_ignore ();
    peg_detail::line_col_at (in.source, begin, m.line, m.column);
    return m;
  });
}
\end{code}

The adapter does \emph{not} fire the rule's stored semantic action. The
match record is returned to the caller, who decides what to do with it.
This makes \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}rule} suitable for combinator-style
grammars where the actions are attached to the combinator structure
rather than to the rules. A grammar that wants both the rule's action
and the combinator's structure must call the action explicitly from the
combinator's continuation.

The \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}channel} combinator assigns a channel to a
parser's result. It takes a \texttt{PEGChannel} (or a channel name
string) and a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}, and
returns a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
whose \texttt{channel} and \texttt{ignored} fields are set from the
supplied channel. This is the combinator-side analogue of setting a
rule's \texttt{channel} field in the rule-based engine.

\section{Worked Examples}\label{ch28-worked-examples}

This section assembles the pieces into running programs. Each example is
complete enough to compile with \texttt{-std=c++20} against the single
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} header.

\subsection{Whitespace and Keywords}\label{ch28-whitespace-and-keywords}

The first example extends the pattern of example 23 with a derived
grammar and a custom channel. The base grammar recognises \texttt{if}
and \texttt{then} as keywords and
\texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}][a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]*} as an
identifier. Whitespace is on \texttt{@IGNORE}. The derived grammar adds
a hex literal rule on a custom \texttt{@HEX} channel.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int
main ()
{
  auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();
  auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t\\n]+"> (
      [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "skip_ws:" << m.length () << "\n"; });
  ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;

  auto &kw_if = grammar.add_rule<"if"> (
      [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "kw:" << m.value << "\n"; });
  auto &kw_then = grammar.add_rule<"then"> (
      [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "kw:" << m.value << "\n"; });
  auto &ident = grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"> (
      [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "id:" << m.value << "\n"; });

  auto result = grammar.parse ("if flag then");
  std::cout << (result.ok () ? "parse_ok" : "parse_failed") << "\n";
  std::cout << "offset=" << result.offset << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The output shows the action firing order: \texttt{kw:\allowbreak{}if},
\texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ws:\allowbreak{}1},
\texttt{id:\allowbreak{}flag},
\texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ws:\allowbreak{}1},
\texttt{kw:\allowbreak{}then}. The ignore action fires alongside the
keyword actions, demonstrating that the channel suppresses token
emission but not action invocation. The final
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}ok} and \texttt{offset=11} confirm that the
whole input was consumed.

\subsection{A Number and Identifier
Grammar}\label{ch28-a-number-and-identifier-grammar}

The second example uses the combinator family to build a tokenizer that
distinguishes signed integers from identifiers. It mirrors example 22
but adds an action that collects tokens into a vector. The
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}choice} combinator tries the integer rule
first; if it matches, the identifier alternative is not tried at that
position.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <cctype>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Token
{
  std::string kind;
  std::string text;
};

int
main ()
{
  std::vector<Token> tokens;
  auto emit = [&] (std::string kind) {
    return [&] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) {
      tokens.push_back ({ std::move (kind), std::string (m.value) });
    };
  };

  auto digit = dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c) { return std::isdigit (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)); },
      "digit");
  auto alpha = dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c) { return std::isalpha (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)); },
      "alpha");
  auto alnum = dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c) { return std::isalnum (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)); },
      "alnum");

  auto integer = dsl::peg_seq (dsl::peg_opt (dsl::ch ('+') | dsl::ch ('-')),
                               dsl::peg_many1 (digit));
  auto identifier = dsl::peg_seq (dsl::peg_and (alpha),
                                 dsl::peg_many1 (alnum));
  auto token = dsl::peg_choice (integer, identifier);

  auto r = dsl::run_parser (dsl::peg_many (token), "42 abc -7");
  std::cout << "ok=" << (r.value ? "yes" : "no") << "\n";
  for (const auto &t : tokens)
    std::cout << t.kind << ":" << t.text << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}choice} ordering matters: placing
\texttt{integer} before \texttt{identifier} means that a leading digit
is parsed as a number, never as the start of an identifier. Reversing
the order would parse \texttt{42} as an identifier-like token (failing,
because
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many1(\allowbreak{}alnum)\allowbreak{}}
accepts digits), changing the token stream. This is the ordered-choice
rule in action: the parse is determined by alternative order, not by
longest match.

\subsection{Ordered Choice and Rule
Order}\label{ch28-ordered-choice-and-rule-order}

The third example demonstrates ordered choice at the rule level. Two
grammars are constructed that differ only in the order of their rules.
The first places a keyword rule before the identifier rule; the second
reverses them. The same input produces different token streams from the
two grammars.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int
main ()
{
  auto g1 = dsl::create_peg_definition ();
  g1.add_rule<"if"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "kw:" << m.value << "\n"; });
  g1.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_]+"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "id:" << m.value << "\n"; });

  auto g2 = dsl::create_peg_definition ();
  g2.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_]+"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "id:" << m.value << "\n"; });
  g2.add_rule<"if"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "kw:" << m.value << "\n"; });

  std::cout << "--- g1 ---\n";
  g1.parse ("if");
  std::cout << "--- g2 ---\n";
  g2.parse ("if");
}
\end{code}

The first grammar prints \texttt{kw:\allowbreak{}if} because the keyword
rule appears first and matches. The second grammar prints
\texttt{id:\allowbreak{}if} because the identifier rule appears first
and matches the same input. Neither grammar is wrong; they express
different intents. The lesson is that PEG rule order is part of the
grammar's specification, not an implementation detail.

\subsection{Capturing Nested
Structure}\label{ch28-capturing-nested-structure}

The fourth example uses the \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} hook to
capture structure that spans more than one rule. A rule matching an
opening bracket fires a hook that attempts to match the body and the
closing bracket. The hook's action records the inner span; the primary
action records the outer span.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int
main ()
{
  auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();

  auto &open = grammar.add_rule<"("> (
      [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "open@" << m.begin << "\n"; });
  auto &body = grammar.add_rule<"[a-z]+"> (
      [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "body:" << m.value << "\n"; });
  auto &close = grammar.add_rule<")"> (
      [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "close@" << m.begin << "\n"; });
  auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t]*"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
  ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;

  auto result = grammar.parse ("(abc)");
  std::cout << (result.ok () ? "ok" : "fail") << "\n";
}
\end{code}

This example does not use \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} directly;
instead it relies on the rule list itself expressing the sequence
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}}, body, \texttt{)\allowbreak{}} through repeated
application of ordered choice at successive positions. The driver
advances the cursor after each match, so the next rule is tried at the
next position. This is the token-stream model: the grammar describes
individual tokens, and structure emerges from their order in the stream.

For genuinely nested structure, the combinator family is more
expressive.
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}seq(\allowbreak{}open,\allowbreak{} peg\_\allowbreak{}many(\allowbreak{}body)\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} close)\allowbreak{}}
parses a bracketed group in one combinator expression, and
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many} of that expression parses nested groups.
The rule-based engine does not directly express nesting because each
rule is applied independently at the current position; nesting requires
the combinator layer.

\section{Pitfalls}\label{ch28-pitfalls}

\subsection{Ordered Choice Is Not Longest
Match}\label{ch28-ordered-choice-is-not-longest-match}

PEG ordered choice takes the first alternative that matches, not the
longest. A grammar that places a shorter alternative before a longer one
will always take the shorter, even when the longer would have consumed
more input. This is the most common source of surprise for authors
coming from regex or from longest-match parser generators.

The classic example is a keyword rule placed before an identifier rule.
The keyword rule matches \texttt{if} and the cursor advances by two; the
identifier rule, which would have matched \texttt{if} as well, is never
tried. This is usually the desired behaviour for keywords. The failure
mode appears when the shorter rule matches a prefix of a longer token: a
keyword \texttt{in} placed before an identifier rule will match the
\texttt{in} of \texttt{integer}, leaving \texttt{teger} to be parsed by
the next rule. The fix is to make the keyword rule require a
non-identifier follower, using a negated lookahead or a boundary class.

\subsection{Left Recursion Is Not
Supported}\label{ch28-left-recursion-is-not-supported}

A PEG rule that begins with a reference to itself, directly or
indirectly, will loop forever because the engine always tries the rule
at the same position before consuming any input. The library does not
implement left-recursion elimination. A grammar that needs left
recursion must be rewritten to use right recursion or an explicit loop.

In the rule-based engine, left recursion manifests as an infinite loop
in \texttt{parse}, because the cursor never advances. In the combinator
engine, it manifests as a stack overflow when the parser recurses
without consuming input. Neither engine detects the situation; the
author must structure the grammar to avoid it.

The standard rewrite for left-recursive rules like
\texttt{E -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} E '+' T | T} is to factor the
recursion into a repetition:
\texttt{E -\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} T (\allowbreak{}'+' T)\allowbreak{}*}.
The \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many} combinator expresses this directly.
The rule-based engine expresses it through repeated application of
ordered choice at successive positions, which is equivalent to a
repetition when the grammar is structured as a flat token stream.

\subsection{Ignore Rules Must Be Ordered
Correctly}\label{ch28-ignore-rules-must-be-ordered-correctly}

An ignore rule that can match the same input as a semantic rule will
shadow the semantic rule if it appears first in the rule list. Because
\texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ignored} in the \texttt{PEGMatcher} runs
before the arm list, ignore rules always run first in the matcher path;
this is correct behaviour for whitespace. In the \texttt{parse} driver,
ignore rules run in their declaration order alongside semantic rules, so
an ignore rule placed before a semantic rule that matches the same input
will suppress the semantic match.

The fix is to make ignore rules specific: a whitespace rule
\texttt{[ \textbackslash{}t\textbackslash{}n]+} does not overlap with an
identifier rule \texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}]+}, so their relative
order does not matter. An ignore rule that matches a broad class like
\texttt{.\allowbreak{}*} would shadow everything and is almost certainly
a bug.

\subsection{Empty-Match Rules Loop Forever Under
Repetition}\label{ch28-empty-match-rules-loop-forever-under-repetition}

A rule that can match the empty string, placed under
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many} or repeated by the parse driver, will
loop forever because the cursor does not advance. The parse driver
detects this and returns an explicit error. The combinator engine does
not detect it;
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many(\allowbreak{}p)\allowbreak{}} where
\texttt{p} can match the empty string will loop until the stack
overflows.

The fix is to ensure that every repetition consumes at least one
character on each iteration. A pattern like \texttt{[a-z]*} should be
\texttt{[a-z]+} when used as a repetition body. A pattern that genuinely
matches the empty string, such as an optional clause inside a
repetition, should be restructured so that the repetition body cannot
succeed without consuming input.

The \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many1} combinator enforces this for its
first iteration by requiring at least one match. The
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many} combinator does not, because zero
iterations is a valid result for \texttt{a*}. Both assume that the body,
when it does match, consumes input; this is the author's responsibility.

\section{Summary}\label{ch28-summary}

The PEG engine provides two execution paths over the same grammar.
\texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse} is the
all-in-one driver: it walks the input, applies ordered choice across all
visible rules at each position, fires actions, and reports success or
failure with line and column information. The \texttt{PEGMatcher} is the
fluent path: it advances one arm at a time, supports wildcard recovery,
and offers channel filtering for selective token enumeration. Both paths
share \texttt{PEGMatch} as their reporting record and both honour the
channel discipline that routes ignore matches silently while still
firing their actions.

The combinator family \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}seq},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}choice}, \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}opt},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many}, \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}many1},
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}and}, and \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}not}
re-expresses the same PEG semantics through the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Parser} machinery. The
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}rule} and \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}channel}
adapters bridge the rule-based and combinator-based worlds, allowing a
grammar to mix compiled pattern rules with combinator expressions in a
single program. The combinators implement strict PEG semantics: ordered
choice with full backtracking on partial failure, greedy repetition that
never commits on partial input, and zero-width lookahead for
context-sensitive assertions.

The engine's semantics are unforgiving in the ways that matter. Ordered
choice is first-match, not longest-match. Left recursion is not
supported. Empty-match rules under repetition are detected and rejected
by the parse driver, but the combinator engine trusts the author to
avoid them. Ignore rules must be specific enough not to shadow semantic
rules. These constraints are the price of the engine's simplicity and
predictability; a grammar that respects them parses deterministically
and reports its failures precisely.

\section{Looking Back: The Whole
Toolkit}\label{ch28-looking-back-the-whole-toolkit}

This chapter closes the 28-chapter tour of MuDSL. The toolkit began in
Chapter 1 with a design philosophy: embedded DSLs expressed as C++20
libraries, header-only, namespace-\texttt{dsl}, built on the CRTP
foundation of Chapter 3. It proceeded through compile-time strings
(Chapter 4), feature tags and mixins (Chapter 5), the pipeline and
operator features (Chapters 6 and 7), and the pattern-matching and
compile-time regex facilities of Chapters 8 and 9. The pattern regex of
Chapter 9 reappears here as the compiled-pattern engine inside
\texttt{PEGRule}, the same regex subset powering both the static
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
matcher and the runtime PEG rules.

The AST layer of Chapters 10 through 14 provided the value type, the
tree builders, the traversal and dumping machinery, and the rewrite
engine with its fixpoint optimisation. The expression templates of
Chapters 15 and 16 made trees lazy and fused their evaluation. Custom
literals (Chapter 17), memoization (Chapter 18),
\texttt{Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 19),
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 20), the monadic optional helpers (Chapter 21), and
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 22) supplied the supporting infrastructure that makes embedded
DSLs ergonomic and safe.

The parser layer of Chapters 23 through 25 introduced the combinator
framework, the primitive parsers, the diagnostic system, and the
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} driver. Chapters 26 and 27 moved from
parsing into task pipelines and the PEG definition grammar. This chapter
unified the two: the PEG engine's rule-based path and the
combinator-based path share the same \texttt{PEGMatch} record, the same
channel discipline, and the same PEG semantics. A grammar author can
choose the rule-based path for its runtime flexibility and the
combinator path for its static composability, or mix the two through the
\texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}rule} adapter.

The thread that runs through all 28 chapters is the toolkit's commitment
to composition. Features compose through mixins. Parsers compose through
combinators. Patterns compose through the regex subset. PEG rules
compose through ordered choice and the \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed}
hook. The same \texttt{PEGMatch} record serves both the rule-based and
combinator-based PEG engines. The same
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Parser} machinery serves both the
standalone parser combinators and the \texttt{peg\_\allowbreak{}*}
family. A DSL built on MuDSL is itself a composition of these
facilities, assembled into a header that reads like a grammar and
compiles like a C++ program.
